## 1️⃣ Add a solid “catch‑all” error‑handling layer

Express will only invoke an error‑handling middleware when `next(err)` (or an uncaught exception in an async route) is called.  Right now the app has none, so any unexpected exception would crash the process or leak a stack trace to the client.

```js
// --- after all route definitions, before app.listen() ---

// 1️⃣  Generic async wrapper (optional but handy)
const asyncWrap = fn => (req, res, next) => {
  Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);
};

// Example usage (later you can replace app.get with asyncWrap)
app.get('/users', asyncWrap(async (req, res) => {
  // imagine DB call here...
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
  ]);
}));

// 2️⃣  Central error‑handling middleware
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  // Log the error (you can swap this out for winston/pino/etc.)
  console.error(`[${new Date().toISOString()}]`, err);

  // Do **not** expose internal details in production
  const isProd = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production';
  const status = err.statusCode || 500;
  const message = isProd ? 'Internal Server Error' : err.message;

  res.status(status).json({ error: message });
});
```

* **Why:**  
  * Guarantees the process won’t crash on an uncaught exception.  
  * Keeps stack traces out of responses when `NODE_ENV=production`.  
  * Central point for logging, alerting, or forwarding errors to a monitoring service.

