## 2️⃣ Validate any future user‑supplied data (query, body, params)

Even though the current routes don’t accept input, it’s a good habit to scaffold validation now.  The most popular, lightweight option is **Joi** (or **@hapi/joi**).  You can also use **express‑validator** if you prefer a middleware‑centric style.

```bash
npm i joi        # or npm i @hapi/joi
```

```js
// validators/users.js
const Joi = require('joi');

exports.paginationSchema = Joi.object({
  page: Joi.number().integer().min(1).default(1),
  limit: Joi.number().integer().min(1).max(100).default(20),
});
```

```js
// In a route that will later accept pagination params
const { paginationSchema } = require('./validators/users');

app.get(
  '/users',
  asyncWrap(async (req, res, next) => {
    const { error, value: query } = paginationSchema.validate(req.query);
    if (error) {
      // Throw a 400 – Bad Request; caught by our error handler
      const err = new Error(`Invalid query: ${error.details[0].message}`);
      err.statusCode = 400;
      throw err;
    }

    // Use query.page / query.limit safely…
    res.json(/* … */);
  })
